with replication enabled (wal_level=replica, replication role, listening for standbys)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"initdb ran successfully, postgresql.conf set wal_level=replica/max_wal_senders/hot_standby/listen_addresses='*', pg_hba.conf added scram-sha-256 rules for the replicator role and app traffic, a replicator role with REPLICATION LOGIN was created, and the service started and listened on 5432 (confirmed again in the after-executor lifecycle snapshot).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-45f30d52a27c416d\",\"cmd-08d6dd16b9ff4fa4\",\"cmd-27bcaa4ee7684a31\",\"cmd-f3eea359bebb456d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-standbys\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 configured as read-only standbys streaming from node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"pg_basebackup -R was run from node1 on both node2 and node3, producing standby.signal and primary_conninfo automatically; both services started and pg_stat_replication on node1 showed both peers in 'streaming' state; pg_is_in_recovery()=t confirmed on both standbys.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b57d250cef3547c9\",\"cmd-761f65fe5cfe4a63\",\"cmd-bad3eb7042e243d4\",\"cmd-265dcced97f441b5\",\"cmd-3e2e4f80a77444db\",\"cmd-6c316d5801324628\",\"cmd-c8edc65ebcd2425a\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-replication-and-readonly\",\"requirement\":\"Changes committed on the primary are visible on both standbys, and standbys are read-only\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An INSERT on node1 (live-commit-test) appeared on both node2 and node3 within ~1 second; direct INSERT attempts on both standbys were rejected with 'cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction'. This was independently re-verified after the full restart cycle with a second INSERT (post-restart-check) also propagating to both standbys.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9a56e2e8bcd745a1\",\"cmd-cad2f2301a0e4861\",\"cmd-80a7617402934b12\",\"cmd-91706aee201c4a10\",\"cmd-d3d7cd2643b54790\",\"cmd-a30308d06ede45ec\",\"cmd-77382027dacf4480\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4-restart-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: each node shows pre-restart healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each node's boot_id, uptime, and PostgreSQL/replication state were captured before a scheduled reboot; connectivity was lost and re-established with a new boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot boot-identity diffs for all three nodes); after each reboot, postgresql auto-started, standby recovery/pg_is_in_recovery and full baseline+live data were confirmed, and node1's post-reboot streaming to both standbys plus a fresh INSERT replicating to both was verified. Standbys were rebooted first, primary last, per the intended order.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c84e396c533d4126\",\"cmd-9fb7a170657b4e73\",\"cmd-cecbf21000f4430b\",\"cmd-e51b72a2673c458a\",\"cmd-4249279437e248cb\",\"cmd-9474df237b3844fc\",\"cmd-65dfe10a62ab44a1\",\"cmd-3d95f1bd3ab1427e\",\"cmd-72cfdb95e1a44b84\",\"cmd-bb09c30a37c74d08\",\"cmd-647dadeb06d44d65\",\"cmd-a6e68ff823784946\",\"cmd-06bf038f2f1e42cc\",\"cmd-d3d7cd2643b54790\",\"cmd-a30308d06ede45ec\",\"cmd-77382027dacf4480\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"The executor temporarily unlocked root SSH login (editing /etc/shadow to allow pubkey auth) on node2 and node3 solely to scp a replication password file from node1, since no other secret-distribution channel was available; this weakens a security control, but it was bounded (pubkey-only, no password auth enabled since sshd_config kept 'prohibit-password'), fully reverted (shadow re-locked, keys/authorized_keys removed) on all three nodes (cmd-de77257bfbfd4f1d, cmd-63fe4eb099044b2d, cmd-c16c218857b9467f), and no lingering key material or root access remained in the after-executor snapshot. Two earlier troubleshooting attempts (an ad-hoc python3 http.server for secret transfer, and an openssl-based password generator) failed outright (python3/openssl not installed) and left no residue since the workaround (scp) succeeded and /tmp/xfer was removed (cmd-a73c65b05b884950, cmd-cdcd7f699d54468c, cmd-8535afaaf0b94b53 openssl attempt, cmd-c16c218857b9467f cleanup). Minor leftover artifacts /tmp/apk.log and /tmp/reboot.log remain on all three nodes per the after-executor snapshot; these are low-risk incidental log files from legitimate install/reboot actions, not actively harmful, but were not cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6f96244451b14e10\",\"cmd-120d6ffbeb06454c\",\"cmd-704997597d6546cb\",\"cmd-de77257bfbfd4f1d\",\"cmd-63fe4eb099044b2d\",\"cmd-c16c218857b9467f\",\"cmd-a73c65b05b884950\",\"cmd-cdcd7f699d54468c\",\"cmd-8535afaaf0b94b53\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task are directly satisfied by evidence: node1 is a properly configured writable primary, node2 and node3 are streaming, read-only standbys built via pg_basebackup -R, and committed writes on the primary are shown propagating to both standbys while direct writes to the standbys are rejected. The additional provider-wide restart protocol was executed correctly for all three nodes in the intended standbys-first/primary-last order, with boot-identity changes cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots and full replication/data recovery confirmed after each reboot, including a post-restart write that also replicated successfully. The only notable hygiene concern is a temporary, bounded, and fully reverted root-SSH unlock used to transfer the replication password between nodes, plus small non-harmful leftover log files in /tmp.\",\"limitations\":[\"TLS/SSL for replication traffic was not configured or verified (sslmode=prefer was left at PostgreSQL's default), so encryption-in-transit of replication data was not assessed.\",\"The password used for the replicator role was generated and transferred out-of-band; its value and secure storage details were not independently verifiable beyond the executor's own account.\",\"Restart evidence relies on the executor's own polling loop for reachability; no fully independent third-party confirmation of downtime bounds was available beyond the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots, which do corroborate the reported boot-id changes.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-primary","requirement":"node1 configured as a writable PostgreSQL primary with replication enabled (wal_level=replica, replication role, listening for standbys)","status":"satisfied","summary":"initdb ran successfully, postgresql.conf set wal_level=replica/max_wal_senders/hot_standby/listen_addresses='*', pg_hba.conf added scram-sha-256 rules for the replicator role and app traffic, a replicator role with REPLICATION LOGIN was created, and the service started and listened on 5432 (confirmed again in the after-executor lifecycle snapshot).","evidence_ids":["cmd-45f30d52a27c416d","cmd-08d6dd16b9ff4fa4","cmd-27bcaa4ee7684a31","cmd-f3eea359bebb456d","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2-standbys","requirement":"node2 and node3 configured as read-only standbys streaming from node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"pg_basebackup -R was run from node1 on both node2 and node3, producing standby.signal and primary_conninfo automatically; both services started and pg_stat_replication on node1 showed both peers in 'streaming' state; pg_is_in_recovery()=t confirmed on both standbys.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b57d250cef3547c9","cmd-761f65fe5cfe4a63","cmd-bad3eb7042e243d4","cmd-265dcced97f441b5","cmd-3e2e4f80a77444db","cmd-6c316d5801324628","cmd-c8edc65ebcd2425a"]},{"id":"req-3-repli